#>              %a7Chooses the next board, following in alphabetical order.

#<              %a7Chooses the next board, preceding in alphabetical order.
#               %a7(Opposite to %a1>%a7)

#+              %a7Chooses the next board, following  in alphabetical order 
#               %a7which contains new entries and
#               %a7activates scanner menu, if switched on in %a1SETup%a7.
#               %a7(additionally refer to : %a1SCAnner%a7)

#-              %a7Chooses the next board, preceding in alphabetical order
#               %a7which contains new entries.
#               %a7(Opposite to %a1+%a7)

#?              %a7Displays all commands, which are momentary available for your
#               %a7level. Within each command all letters are marked which are
#               %a7sufficient to recognise the command (these are either in 
#               %a7capital letters, or white coloured).

#ANSI           %a7Turns ANSI emulation (Colour and cursor control) on, or off.

#ADDRess        %a7Enables you to change the following personal data:
#               %a7- address
#               %a7- telephone number
#               %a7- used computer
#               %a7- used modem

#APPlication    %a7Changes you from a guest to an enrolled user of this BBS.

#ANSWer    <Nr> %a7Enables you to write a comment to the mail %a5<Nr> %a7in the
#               %a7same board, in which the mail is located.
#               %a7It operates in ASCII boards only.
#               %a7(identical to %a3R)eply %a7in read menu)

#CApacity       %a7A graphical display of the amount of calls, the whole on-line
#               %a7time in minutes, and the percentage call capacity of the BBS
#               %a7in the last 14 days.

#OBtain         %a7Causes the momentary new mails to be still new at your next call
#               %a7Can be turned off by a 2nd call up of command %a1BEzug %a7.

#BOXstatus      %a7Displays informations according the momentary BBS status
#               %a7(e.g. whether the sysop is available).

#%a3Board                   %a6---------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#Board          %a7Shows all boards with new entries available since your last
#               %a7login. When you are in a parent board, all of its daughter  
#               %a7boards are displayed only.
#Board *        %a7Displays all boards available for you.
#               %a7When you are in a parent board, all of its daughter boards are
#               %a7displayed only.
#Board **       %a7Identical to %a1Brett *%a7 with the own difference: 
#               %a7you will every time see the boards of the main menu nevertheless
#               %a7you are in a parent board or not.
#Board <Name>   %a7Chooses the board which is identified by %a5<Name> %a7. 
#               %a7It is not necessary to enter the complete board name.
#               %a7When the wanted board is located in a parent menu, the input of
#               %a7the name of the wanted board is sufficient WITHOUT input of the
#               %a7name of the parent board.
#               %a7During input you can resign to enter command %a2Brett %a7, if
#               %a7the wanted board name does not conflict with a command.
#               %a7In conflicting case the command will be called up instead.
#Board ..       %a7If you are in a daughter board you reach the parent board again.
#               %a7If you are in the highest parent board or in a normal board you 
#               %a7reach your PM. I.e. every time you climb one board level up. 
#Board #        %a7Activates the menu driven board selection. You can resign to
#               %a7enter command %a2Brett %a7again. %a1#%a7(Return) is sufficient.
#Board ?        %a7Outputs a list of all boards available for you with their 
#               %a7comments.
#Board #NEU     %a7Installs a new board (for sysop and cosysop only)
#Board #ALT     %a7Deletes the actual board (for sysop and cosysop only)

#DAte           %a7To enter your relation date.
#               %a7All entries, which are written after that date, are now marked
#               %a7for you as new.

#DIalogue       %a7Asks the sysop for a conversation (Chat) or gives you the
#               %a7possibility to leave the sysop a message, when he is not avai-
#               %a7lable right now.

#%a3EDit                   %a6---------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#EDit <No>      %a7If you are in an ASCII board, you can change herewith the text
#               %a7with the number %a5<Nr> %a7which you have written before by your
#               %a7own.
#               %a7In binary boards you can change the name and commentary of the
#               %a7file with the number %a5<Nr>%a7, which you have sent before.
#EDit BRETT     %a7To edit the actual board (for sysop/cosysop only)
#EDit USER      %a7To edit an enrolled user (for sysop/cosysop only)
#EDit NETZ      %a7To edit net BBS entries  (for sysop/cosysop only)

#End            %a7Takes you back to main menu (PM) or, if you are just there,
#               %a7it will call up command %a1LOGoff%a7.

#Extern <No>    %a7Will call up the external program with the number %a5<Nr> %a7.
#               %a7If you enter the command without a number, you will enter
#               %a7the selection menu, which shows all available external 
#               %a7programs with their short descriptions.
#               %a7Most of the time you will find ,games or statistical programs
#               %a7there in.

#Guidedtour     %a7A guided tour which shows the most important commands and their
#               %a7usage. The tour can be cancelled before time by entering 
#               %a1Fuehrung %a7 a second time.

#MANufacturer   %a7Displays the manufacturer and the serial number of the BBS 
#               %a7program.

#HElp <Command> %a7Shows a help text for the command %a5<Befehl> %a7.
#               %a7If the parameter %a5<Befehl> %a7 is omitted, a help text for
#               %a7all commands of the BBS will be displayed.

#%a3Contents                 %a6---------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#Contents       %a7Display of all new entries in a board, or, if you are in a
#               %a7parent board, you get a display of all its daughter boards which
#               %a7contain new entries.
#               %a7(identical to %a3(C)ontents %a7in scanner menu)
#Contents *     %a7Display of all entries in a board, or, if you are in a 
#               %a7parent board, you get a display of all its daughter boards.
#               %a7(identical to %a3(W)hole_Contents %a7in scanner menu)
#Contents x-    %a7Display of all entries of a board starting with number %a5x %a7.
#Contents -x    %a7Display of all entries of a board up to the number %a5x %a7.
#Contents x-y   %a7Display of all entries of a board from  %a5x %a7up to %a5y %a7.
#Contents <User>%a7Display of all entries of user %a5<User> %a7in a binary board.
#Contents #     %a7Activates the cursor driven message selection.
#               %a7(can be activated automatically after %a1Brett # %a7, if set in %a1SETup%a7)
#               %a7(identical to %a3(#) %a7in scanner menu)
#               %a7All variations of command %a1Inhalt %a7are usable in the 
#               %a7letterbox (PM) with the exception of %a1Inhalt <User> %a7.

#%a3Read                  %a6---------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#Read           %a7Reads all new entries in a board.
#               %a7(identical to %a3(R)ead %a7in Scannermenu)
#Read *         %a7Reads all entries of a board.
#Read <No>      %a7Reads the entry number %a5<Nr>%a7 in a board.
#Read x,y,z     %a7Reads the entries number %a5x%a7, %a5y %a7and %a5z %a7in a board.
#Read x-y       %a7Reads the entries number %a5x %a7up to %a5y %a7in a board.
#Read -x        %a7Reads the entries number 1 up to %a5x %a7in a board.
#Read x-        %a7Reads the entries number %a5x %a7up to the last entry in a board.
#Read merken    %a7Will do a batch download of the binary file(s) noticed via
#               %a1Merken %a7.
#               %a7All %a1Lesen %a7commands with parameters are identical to
#               %a3(O)nly_Read %a7in scanner menu except %a1Lesen merken%a7.
#               %a7The parameters, which have to be entered there, are the same.
#               %a7Of course, all parameters can be entered in combination,
#               %a7e.g. : %a2Lesen x-,y,-z,a-b%a7.
#               %a7An exclamation mark %a5! %a7postponed to the parameter(s) means in
#               %a7in binary boards, that the entries identified by the parameters
#               %a7are transfered via batch download.
#               %a7An postponed exclamation mark %a5!%a7 means in ASCII boards, that the
#               %a7ASCII mails identified by the parameters should be downloaded.
#               %a7Reading of binary file with postponed %a5! %a7is NOT possible in
#               %a7ASCII boards as well as in the letter box (PM).
#               %a7An additional hash mark %a5#%a7 following the read statement in binary
#               %a7boards will cancel the connection after transfer of the data.
#               %a7With the exception of %a1Lesen merken %a7 the command %a2Lesen %a7 can be
#               %a7omitted.

#LIst <NO>      %a7Lists all files which are stored in the archive with the
#               %a7number %a5<Nr.>%a7. An archive is defined as a file which can
#               %a7be extracted in singled files using a decrunching program
#               %a7(like Arc, DMs, LZh, LhA, OWS, Shrink, Zoo or Zoom in
#               %a7accordance to the file suffix).
#               %a7Archive files with the following suffixes can be listed by
#               %a1LIst%a7-command : %a2.ARC .LZH .LHA .SHR .ZOO .TXT .DOC

#DELete         %a7Deletes entries in a board or letterbox (PM).
#               %a7The %a1LOEschen%a7-command understands the same parameters as the
#               %a1Lesen%a7-command, with the exception of the postponed %a5#%a7.
#               %a7But, a postponed %a5! %a7means herein, that the entry will be deleted
#               %a7physically but will be shown in the board contents list with the
#               %a7remark %a2DEL %a7 nevertheless.
#               %a7The %a5!%a7-function is not possible in the letterbox (PM).
#               %a7In boards you can delete only entries you have generated!

#LOGoff [neu]   %a7Will bring you out of the BBS. If you do not enter %a1BEzug
#               %a7before, all entries in boards and your letterbox (PM) are set
#               %a7now to status old for you. But, if you enter the parameter %a5neu%a7,
#               %a7it has the same effect as if you have entered command %a1Bezug%a7
#               %a7before leaving the BBS.

#%a3MArk                  %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#MArk +/- <b>   %a7Marks (resp. unmarks) the board with the name %a5<b>%a7.
#               %a7To mark a board means, that that board will not be shown as new,
#               %a7even if it contains new entries.
#               %a7If you mark a parent board, all of its daughter boards will be
#               %a7marked too.
#               %a7e.g. MARK + Z-NETZ                 marks   the board Z-NETZ
#               %a7e.g. MARK - Z-NETZ                 unmarks the board Z-NETZ
#Notice         %a7Notices binary files in boards or the letterbox (PM).
#               %a7(identical to %a3(N)otice %a7in scanner menu)
#               %a7The %a1Merken%a7-command understands the same parameter as the
#               %a1Lesen%a7 command with the exception of a postponed %a5! %a7or %a5# %a7.
#               %a7To download all noticed files via batch download later on you
#               %a7only need to enter command %a1Lesen merken %a7.

#ANnouncement   %a7For editing a text line, which everybody see, who is writing a
#               %a7letter to you.

#MOde           %a7activates the command menu. In this menu you can call up
#               %a7box commands by pressing only one key for your convenience.

#%a3MUltiuser              %a6---------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#MUltiuser      %a7Displays who are logged in, on which port, using which connection
#               %a7speeds, and what they are doing.
#MUltiuser s    %a7Enables you to send a user on a different port a mail.
#MUltiuser c    %a7Ask the user on a different port for a conversation (Chat).
#               %a7In the chat mode some commands are available. By command %a1.?%a7
#               %a7within the chat mode you will get a help for these commands.
#Multiuser h    %a7Will make you "invisible" for user on different ports using
#               %a7the %a1MUltiuser%a7-command.
#               %a7You will become "visible" again by input of %a1MUltiuser h %a7again.
#               %a7News or chat requests are displayed to a different user only,
#               %a7when target user has finished a running command.

#Net            %a7Displays a list of all boxes to which users you can send mails
#               %a7from within this BBS (refer to %a1Senden%a7).
#Net <Box>      %a7for level 9 - the chosen BBS %a5<Box>%a7 will be called after logoff.

#PAssword       %a7To change your password.

#PM             %a7Takes you back to your letterbox (PM) from where ever you are.

#PRotocol       %a7Displays who was today at which time, who long and with which 
#               %a7transfer speed in this BBS. Additionally the number of up- and
#               %a7downloads is shown.

#RELogin        %a7You can login again without calling again.
#               %a7This command is very convenient if you have a different person
#               %a7beside you, who wants to enter the BBS using his own name.

#DIrection      %a7Will convert the display direction of the entries in all boards 
#               %a7or your letterbox (PM). You can change the contents listing 
#               %a7between ascending or reverse time order.

#CALLBACK       %a7The BBS will call back the entered number after %a1LOGoff %a7.

#%a3CIRCletter              %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#CIRCLetter x-y %a7The ASCII mail you are writing via this command will be routed
#               %a7to all users of this BBS, which level is located between %a5x %a7and %a5y%a7.
#CIRCLetter!<g> %a7The ASCII mail you are writing via this command will be routed
#               %a7to all users, which are part of the group %a5<g> %a7.

#%a3DOWNloadinfo               %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#DOWNloadinfo   %a7Displays who has downloaded which file of the momentary active 
#               %a7binary board.
#DOWNloadi kill %a7Deletes the sauginfo information.

#%a3Send                   %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#Send           %a7If you are in a binary board, you can send (upload) a binary 
#               %a7file to this board.
#               %a7If you postpone a hash mark %a1# %a7 to the command the
#               %a7connection will be terminated automatically after the transfer
#               %a7is finished (%a1LOGoff%a7).
#               %a7If you are in an ASCII board, you can send a ASCII mail in
#               %a7this board.When you are in the text editor you can get a help
#               %a7for the editor commands via the input of %a1.? %a7!
#               %a7If you are in your letterbox (PM), you can send a mail to 
#               %a7any user.
#Send   <name>  %a7You can send a mail to the user specified by parameter 
#               %a5<name>%a7 where ever you are in the BBS.
#               %a7A postponed %a1# %a7 will log you off you automatically 
#               %a7after sending this mail.

#SCAnner        %a7Will select the next board in alphabetical order, that contains
#               %a7new entries and starts up the scanner menu (if set in 
#               %a1SETup %a7).
#               %a7In the scanner menu you will get a help by input of %a1? %a7for
#               %a7all available scanner commands.
#               %a7The scanner is the common way for experienced users to read fast
#               %a7and easy all news within the BBS.

#SETup          %a7Lets you set your personal BBS configuration via a menu. 
#               %a7Just go to all menu items and choose your most convenient setup.
#               %a2Modem protocol %a7means the kind of transfer protocol for binary 
#               %a7files.
#               %a7The common and most convenient setup is %a2ZModem 1K%a7.
#               %a7If you switch on the %a2Status line%a7, you get a display of 
#               %a7the actual time, date, rest of your online time, and the actual 
#               %a7telephone fee in German Pfennigs.
#SIgnatur       %a7Enables you to create and store a signature of maximum 5 lines. 
#               %a7This signature can be copied to your text mails in the online 
#               %a7text editor every time you enter the editor command %a1.s %a7.

#SPeedcheck     %a7Checks and displays the momentary transfer speed of the modem
#               %a7connection.

#STatus         %a7Display of your personal user status.

#SEArch         %a7Possibility to search for a term within a binary board.
#               %a7When you are in your letterbox (PM), all binary boards will be
#               %a7taken for the search.

#SYstemlevel    %a7After input of an additional password you will reach the command
#               %a7shell of the system (for sysops only, of course).

#%a3PARTicipants             %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#Participants   %a7Displays a list of all users with additional information.
#Participants<n>%a7Displays additional information of the user with name %a5<n>%a7.

#REPresent      %a7Gives you the possibility to declare a user within the BBS as
#               %a7your representative. Every letter written to you will be copied
#               %a7to your representative too. The common usage of this command is 
#               %a7to install somebody else during your absence to look for your 
#               %a7personal mails.

#%a3Visitcard             %a6-------- %a3several syntax possible %a6----------
#VIsitcard      %a7Enables you ...
#               %a7- to create a visiting card
#               %a7- to delete your own visiting card
#               %a7- to display yours or somebody else's visiting card.
#VIsitcard n    %aYou can see the visiting card of the user with name %a5n %a7.

#Forward        %a7Will let you pass on (copy) the board entry with the number %a5n %a7
#               %a7to any different board you like. If you want you can delete
#               %a7the file entry from within the source board.

#TIme           %a7Displays how long you are actually online in the BBS and how 
#               %a7long you are still allowed to stay. 
#               %a7(online time and rest of online time).

#ACcess         %a7If you are the manager of the active board you can create and 
#               %a7edit a list of users which can access that board.
#               %a7The sysop or the cosysop can give you the status of a board
#               %a7manager only.
